<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petronas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1974">1974</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Petroliam Nasional Berhad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petronas (Petroliam Nasional Berhad) is Malaysia's state-owned oil and gas company, founded in 1974 and vested with ownership of the country's petroleum resources. Its revenues have been a major source of government income, and it operates internationally in exploration, production and liquefied natural gas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas-twin-towers">Petronas Twin Towers</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuala-lumpur">Kuala Lumpur</a> house its headquarters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petronas.jpg" alt="Petronas" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petronas · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PETRONAS_Logo_(2013).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Oil exploration in the region began before Malaysia existed. Royal Dutch Shell started in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miri">Miri</a>, Sarawak, after Charles Brooke signed the first Oil Mining Lease in 1909, and the first well was drilled there in 1910. Shell was still the only oil company in the area in 1963, when the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> united with Sarawak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> to become Malaysia, and it brought the country's first offshore oilfield into production in 1968.</p><p>Several factors in the early 1970s led the government to found a state-owned company. The oil price rose from US$1.50 a barrel in 1972 to US$12.00 after war in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and an oil embargo by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opec">OPEC</a>. Other countries had adopted production sharing agreements in place of concessions. The government also believed that foreign companies did not inform it properly of discoveries in their concessions, and the New Economic Policy of the early 1970s encouraged Malaysians, and bumiputeras in particular, to take control of modern industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Production-sharing contracts between Petronas and foreign oil companies were signed in 1976. Of the oil produced, 20 per cent went to the companies to recover costs and 10 per cent was paid as royalty, shared equally between the federal and state governments. The remaining 70 per cent, the profit oil, was divided 70:30 between Petronas and the companies, and Petronas did not take over the companies' equity.</p><p>Early in the 21st century the company expanded abroad. In March 2000 it finalised an oil production deal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, and in July 2000 it found oil in the Samarang-Asam Paya contract area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a>. In 2001 it began construction on the Chad-Cameroon Integrated Oil Development and Pipeline Project. It also sponsored the Sauber Formula One team from 1995 to 2009, and then the Mercedes team.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Petronas is a legal entity incorporated under the Malaysian Companies Act 1965, and the federal government is its sole shareholder. Its logo, designed in 1974 by Dato' Johan Ariff, combines an oil drop with the letter P, in emerald green for the sea from which oil and gas are drawn. It is vertically integrated, with exploration, refining, distribution, power generation and trading. Petronas Carigali is its principal exploration and production subsidiary, and the Malaysia Petroleum Management division has overseen the country's upstream assets since the company was established. It also runs a research university, Universiti Teknologi Petronas, built in 1997 at Seri Iskandar in Perak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Petronas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Free trade zones from 1972 drew electronics firms to Penang, the state oil company <b>Petronas</b> was founded in 1974, the national car Proton followed in 1985, and the country became the second-largest producer of palm oil after Indonesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Paphlagonia, a Byzantine army under <b>Petronas</b> encircled and destroyed the forces of Umar al-Aqta, emir of Melitene, who had sacked Amisos on the Black Sea; Umar was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
